+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+Install it with pipx (Python 3.11 or newer): `pipx install tally-toolkit`. Then check it with `tallyctl version`. If
+the command is not found, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release. The old name works as an alias for one more release and
+will then be removed.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731, since the 2026-09 release. The old port 8080 is closed, so anything still using it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear only one. The old purge command was
+removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement. The page goes from the primary to the secondary on-call, then to the engineering
+manager. This replaces the earlier 30-minute rule.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1, which means customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2
+incidents do not page; the primary picks them up in the morning.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Run `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first and look for the cause afterwards. A plain restart does not help, because
+the consumer picks up the same bad batch. The drain parks that batch in the dead-letter table.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Renew it with `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart with `tallyctl restart api`. The service only serves the new
+certificate after the restart. Check expiry dates with `tallyctl certs list`.
